||| Build-time `Linked` instances — one per backend compiled into this
||| `libidrisml` (the `BACKEND` list). The header below is this
||| `HwConfig.idr.in` template; the `Linked` instance block underneath is
||| appended per build by the Makefile's HWCONFIG_IDR recipe.
|||
||| `Linked ex` (declared in `Executor.Core`) gates which device tags are
||| spellable at any constructor carrying the `Linked ex =>` constraint: a
||| torch-only build emits no `Linked (MlxExecutor _)`, so spelling `MlxExecutor`
||| at a constructor fails to typecheck. This is the compile-time
||| *linkage* half of device availability; the runtime
||| *hardware-presence* half is EAFP. See
||| `docs/develop/device-availability-gating.md`.
|||
||| Linkage is per-backend, not per-hardware-variant: a torch build emits
||| `{hw} -> Linked (TorchExecutor hw)`, admitting TCpu / TMps / TCuda n at the
||| type level. Whether that hardware is actually present is the runtime
||| question, answered by attempting construction (EAFP), not by this gate.
|||
||| The `Executor` barrel re-exports this module, so `import Executor` brings
||| the build's `Linked` instances into scope.
|||
||| Do not edit the generated `HwConfig.idr` — it is regenerated on every
||| build and is git-ignored. Edit this `.in` file (the header) or the
||| Makefile HWCONFIG_IDR recipe (the instance block).
module Ml.HwConfig

import public Ml.Executor.Core
import Ml.Executor.Tape
import Ml.Executor.Torch
import Ml.Executor.Mlx

